    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine when the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to identify the values of \\(y\\) that make the denominator zero. The denominator of the expression is \\(y^2 - 5y + 4\\). We start by factoring this quadratic expression.\n\nThe quadratic \\(y^2 - 5y + 4\\) can be factored as follows:\n\\[y^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4).\\]\nThus, the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) can be rewritten as:\n\\[\\frac{y+6}{(y-1)(y-4)}.\\]\nThe expression is undefined when the denominator is zero, i.e., when \\(y - 1 = 0\\) or \\(y - 4 = 0\\). Solving these equations, we get:\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4.\\]\nTherefore, the expression is undefined for \\(y = 1\\) and \\(y = 4\\).\n\nThe sum of all values of \\(y\\) that make the expression undefined is:\n\\[1 + 4 = 5.\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{5}.\\]",
